Found while measuring karr #63 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops). Pre-existing, independent of #63 and of ADR 0020, and JSON does NOT have it.

A bare YAML integer literal with more digits than a double can hold -- 1 followed by 400 zeros -- comes out of YAML::XS as NOK+POK with the NV +Inf. detect_type calls it float, value_to_bytes writes +Inf, and assert_representable refuses it with the karr #59 non-finite guard.

sops writes that document. Measured:

  keep: x
  v_unencrypted: 1000...0   (401 chars)

  sops -e   -> exit 0, the literal reaches the file verbatim
  File::SOPS encrypt of the same tree -> CROAK, "value is a non-finite float (+Inf)"

go-yaml appears to leave a literal that overflows float64 as a STRING rather than resolving it to a number, which is why sops can carry it and we cannot: our parser has already turned it into +Inf before any guard sees it.

The JSON side is the opposite and needs nothing: sops REFUSES such a JSON document at unmarshal time ("strconv.ParseFloat: value out of range", exit 2), so a croak there is the reference behaviour. ADR 0020 notes this as the one place its change makes JSON croak where it used to write a string, and that is correct for JSON.

Open question, and the reason this is low: whether the right answer is to read the literal as a str when Go would (which means modelling go-yaml resolution for the overflow case, next to _go_int and _go_float in Format::YAML), or to accept the divergence and document it. Do not fix by loosening the non-finite guard -- that guard is right about every value it was written for.

Wire lane, with format lane for the resolution model. Needs the sops binary to prove.
